  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Friday...\n\nThe shortwave and associated cold front that dropped through the \narea last night and this morning brought beneficial rainfall to\nportions of the area, especially to the southern mountains and \nfoothills. Some locations in the mountains and foothills received\nnear or more than an inch of liquid precipitation (rain and melted\nsnow), with light snow accumulations above 7,500 ft. Cyclonic flow\nremains over the area, and isolated showers can be expected\nthrough the rest of the afternoon, decreasing into the evening\nhours. As skies clear out tonight, temperatures will drop into the\n30s and 40s, lowest near the foothills. Frost is possible\n(moderate chance) for foothill locations tonight into Sunday\nmorning. Protect sensitive vegetation.\n\nConditions trend drier into Tuesday, outside of occasional pieces\nof energy bringing isolated shower and weak thunderstorm activity\nto the mountains and far east in the afternoons/evenings. By late\nTuesday into Wednesday, a stronger wave is progged to drop through\nnorthwest flow bringing shower/thunderstorm chances and breezy\nwinds. There is currently a moderate chance at 40 mph wind gusts\nfor western foothill locations Tuesday. Best precipitation \npotential is currently for areas north and east of Billings \n(20-60% chance). Occasional pieces of energy will continue to \nmove through the flow through the end of the work week, allowing \nisolated afternoon/evening showers and thunderstorms to continue.\nHeading into the weekend, there are hints that upper troughing\nmoves onto the West Coast, inducing southwest flow over the\nregion, with a better potential for daily thunderstorms. \n\nTemperatures are forecast to range from the 60s to lower 70s\nSunday, to the 70s to around 80 degrees Monday. The warmest day of\nthe week currently looks to be Tuesday with highs in the 70s to\nmiddle 80s, before temperatures cool back into the 70s for most\nareas the remainder of the week. STP",
